Hi Jacqueline,

Thank you for reading the 54 saved replies with me this morning. Please repla=
ce only 06, 12, 21, 25, 29, 37 and 49 with the seven entries in the attached =
CSV; the other 47 can stay as they are. The numbers match your extract so you=
r team can find each entry.

For a case assigned to the Corley English queue, please first state the facts=
 confirmed in the record and their limits. Put the next customer contact in a=
 separate paragraph. Before giving a time, identify the person who will send =
it and confirm that they have accepted the date and time, including across a =
shift change. Write JST with that time. The time is a promise to update, not =
a deadline for resolving the issue. Even if development has not answered by t=
hen, the sender should share what is confirmed and what remains open.

For a case where a domestic colleague is responsible for contacting the custo=
mer, please return the update responsibility to that sender through the domes=
tic duty lead rather than choosing a time for them. The attached PDF explains=
 the distinction with the two examples we read today; the CSV contains the re=
placement text.

Could you switch the seven entries and the duty desk guidance at 10:00 JST on=
 9 January, and remove the duty desk link to the old guidance? Please let me =
know once the change is in place.

Thanks,
Yumika

On Thu, 8 Jan 2026 at 09:18 JST, Jacqueline Howard wrote:
> Hi Yumika,
>
> At the shift change, a new colleague read =E2=80=9CWe'll fix this today=E2=
=80=9D and =E2=80=9CWe'll have this resolved by our next update=E2=80=9D as t=
he same kind of promise: that the issue will be fixed by the time we contact =
the customer again. I can see why the wording sounds that way, but we cannot =
know that from the investigation status alone.
>
> Could we read the attached list of 54 saved replies together from 11:00 JST=
 today? I'd like to separate what Corley can say has actually been confirmed =
from the next contact our team can take responsibility for. We can mark the p=
hrases that suggest more than we know and agree what each should say instead.
>
> Thanks,
> Jacqueline

